title: Two outputs
check: behaves
mode: vhdl
chapter: Arithmetic
---- intro
A circuit can have several outputs: each gets its own line. Here is the half adder you drew, with outputs `sum` and `carry`.

The order of the lines does not matter. Unlike a program, a circuit is not run line by line: all of its gates are there at the same time, working at once.
---- task
Build the half adder: `sum` is `a` XOR `b`, and `carry` is `a` AND `b`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity half_adder is
  port(
    a     : in  std_logic;
    b     : in  std_logic;
    sum   : out std_logic;
    carry : out std_logic
  );
end half_adder;

architecture rtl of half_adder is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity half_adder is
  port(
    a     : in  std_logic;
    b     : in  std_logic;
    sum   : out std_logic;
    carry : out std_logic
  );
end half_adder;

architecture rtl of half_adder is
begin
    sum   <= a xor b;
    carry <= a and b;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two lines, one for each output. The XOR gate's word is `xor`.
---- hint2
`sum <= a xor b;` and `carry <= a and b;`
---- solution
`sum <= a xor b;` and `carry <= a and b;`. In the drawing this took a chip and a gate; here it is two lines.
---- done
Swap the two lines round and nothing changes: they describe two gates that exist side by side. That is the biggest difference between VHDL and a programming language.
